{
	"version": 5,
	"id": "BnM9DFV639MvCf-aY",
	"notes": "NPC creature stat block, not a player-facing race. Point math: Tyranid Biology 20 + Body Lens: Carnifex 1710 (incl. Injury Tolerance 3) + Monstrous Crushing Claws 79 + Venom Cannon 225 + skills 16 = 2050 points. Stats: SM +2, ST 120 (user direction: flips tanks with little struggle), DX 10, IQ 4, HT 16, HP 120, Will 4, Per 4, Basic Move 9, natural chitin torso DR 150 (Weak Points 3, eye DR 75), Regeneration (Fast) — framework.md's Carnifex anchor line exactly. It carries no Shadow in the Warp or synapse role: it is a mindless siege beast. This lands squarely in framework.md's Heroes and Legends tier (1,000-3,000), matching a Hive Tyrant's own physiology cost almost exactly despite the very different build (raw ST and DR rather than IQ, synapse range and psychic power): a living battering ram bred to soak up and deal out damage nothing else on the battlefield can match. The point total is for GM reference only, not party balance.\nWeapon-symbiotes: carries Monstrous Crushing Claws and a Venom Cannon — a classic Carnifex loadout of one melee and one heavy ranged symbiote, both built as natural-attack traits in data/xenos/tyranids/bio-weapons.yaml.\n",
	"traits": [
		{
			"id": "T5TrwWBZtR_s8FChp",
			"name": "Tyranid Biology",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Tyranid Biology shared by every bioform in a Hive Fleet: bound to the Hive Mind from the moment it is grown, disciplined and fearless only so long as a synapse creature's link holds, mute and without individual culture, and adapted to digest or shrug off almost anything a target world can throw at it. This is the baseline every body-type lens below (Termagant through Carnifex) builds on top of; the lens supplies the bioform's own attributes, chitin DR, and any special biology (leaping legs, rending claws, psychic organs, wings) that role calls for.\n\nDesign: 5 (Hive Mind Link) - 10 (Synapse Dependence) + 10 (Synapse Discipline) - 15 (Cannot Speak) + 0 (No Independent Society) + 30 (Adapted Biology) = 20 points of shared physiology before attributes, chitin DR, special biology, or skills. See each template for a full stat block built on top of this plus a body- type lens.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"container_type": "ancestry",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tGaUCD6-kXba14V1W",
					"name": "Hive Mind Link",
					"reference": "B70",
					"local_notes": "A permanent, involuntary telepathic link to the Hive Mind: the organism receives strategic direction, feels the Hive Mind's intent when synapse creatures are near, and cannot meaningfully act against it (see Synapse Dependence and Synapse Discipline below for what that link actually does turn to turn). It carries none of the individual privacy or two-way nuance Mindlink usually implies between two people.\n\nLore: Every Tyranid bioform, however small, is grown already wired into the Hive Mind's collective will — Codex background across editions is explicit that a lone Tyranid organism has essentially no independent will to speak of, and Black Library's Hive Fleet material consistently frames the swarm as a single directed organism wearing a billion bodies rather than a species of individuals.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Mindlink (B70): the published cost table prices the advantage by how many separate people share the link (5 points for a single person, rising with group size). The Hive Mind functions mechanically as one linked entity, not as a separate link to every other Tyranid organism in the swarm, so this project takes the \"single person\" tier (5 points) rather than trying to price a link to billions of individual minds — the same kind of reasoned reinterpretation of a Basic Set category this project's other xenos files use rather than inventing a new, uncosted tier.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "m9p4XtkaTok5OLLOL",
							"name": "Single person (the Hive Mind, treated as one linked entity)",
							"reference": "B70",
							"cost_adj": "5"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tN3piwKV2LtlDwRKr",
					"name": "Synapse Dependence (Instinctive Behaviour)",
					"reference": "B124",
					"local_notes": "Default state: outside synapse range (see Synapse Discipline below), a Tyranid organism reverts to pure instinct. It cannot follow a complex order, hold a defensive position under real pressure, or resist an overwhelming urge to feed on the nearest kill or flee an unwinnable fight — GM's call, biased toward whichever instinct the immediate situation makes obvious (feeding on a fresh kill; fleeing when reduced below 1/3 HP with nowhere to push forward; otherwise holding station near its Hive Fleet's last clear directive). This is fully suppressed while the organism is within the range of a Synapse Creature (see that trait, below) — see Synapse Discipline for what replaces it there.\n\nLore: Codex background is explicit that most Tyranid bioforms have no independent tactical judgement at all: cut off from a synapse creature, a swarm degenerates within moments into a feeding frenzy or a panicked rout, exactly the behaviour Imperial commanders are taught to exploit by killing synapse creatures first. Deathwatch RPG's Mark of the Xenos Tyranid material calibrates this as the single most reliable tactic against a swarm.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Bestial (B124, -10 points): \"you are driven by animal instinct rather than reason,\" applied here as the organism's default, un-linked state rather than an always-on trait, per the Accessibility-style reading this project's own conditional traits (Ork WAAAGH! Gestalt, Necron Slave Mentality) already use for \"the disadvantage's effects only apply in this specific, common-in- play circumstance.\" No further limitation percentage is taken since being outside synapse range is the swarm's normal condition, not a rare edge case — Synapse Discipline below is priced separately as the (comparatively rare, situational) benefit that suppresses it.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"base_points": -10
				},
				{
					"id": "tQWnBUQx22Y8hsdLt",
					"name": "Synapse Discipline",
					"reference": "B55",
					"local_notes": "Fearlessness 5 (+5 to Fright Checks and to Will rolls to resist fear, intimidation and Bestial) and High Pain Threshold (no shock penalty, no stun from injury), both only while within the range of a Synapse Creature's link (see that trait). While linked, Synapse Dependence's instinctive-behaviour effects are fully suppressed: the organism fights with total discipline, no fear, and follows the Hive Mind's tactical intent through the nearest synapse creature exactly as ordered. The moment the link breaks (the synapse creature dies, moves out of range, or is itself cut off from a larger synapse creature further up the chain), Synapse Dependence resumes immediately and the fearlessness and pain immunity go with it.\n\nLore: A swarm fighting under a synapse creature's watch is unrecognisable from the same swarm cut loose: utterly fearless, feeling no pain, perfectly coordinated, and willing to press an attack to the last organism. This is the other half of the same well-documented Codex mechanic that makes synapse creatures the Imperium's priority targets, and matches framework.md's \"no fear or pain within synapse.\"\n\nDesign: Fearlessness 5 (B55, 2 points/level = 10) plus High Pain Threshold (B59, 10) = 20 points, with Accessibility (only while linked to a synapse creature, -50%): 20 x 0.5 = 10 points. Earlier drafts priced a 5-point conditional Will bonus that never delivered the fearlessness and pain immunity the framework and the fiction give Tyranids in synapse.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "m4TptDSCMHqOFGGok",
							"name": "Accessibility (only while linked to a synapse creature)",
							"cost_adj": "-50%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 20
				},
				{
					"id": "t5CFLuoIJjuVxCDvP",
					"name": "Cannot Speak",
					"reference": "B125",
					"local_notes": "A Tyranid organism has no language, no vocal apparatus built for speech, and nothing resembling individual society to speak with — it communicates, if at all, through the Hive Mind Link above, not through sound.\n\nLore: Tyranids are never shown speaking, negotiating, or maintaining any culture separable from the Hive Mind's single collective will — universal, unremarked-on Codex background and consistent across every piece of Tyranid-focussed Black Library fiction.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Cannot Speak (B125), -15 points, unmodified.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"base_points": -15
				},
				{
					"id": "t8adLf6T8VIntNG2y",
					"name": "No Independent Society (Hive Fleet Biology)",
					"local_notes": "0-point lore flag, the same convention this project's Ork Fungal Physiology and Necron Machine-Body entries use: a Tyranid organism has no name, no kin, no culture, and no concept of self outside the Hive Fleet that grew it — grown to purpose in a bio-ship's gestation pools and disassembled for raw biomass on death, not born, raised, or mourned. Every mechanical payoff of this (no language, no independent will outside synapse range, no fear or pain worth the name) is priced separately above and in each body-type lens; this entry exists purely to document why a Tyranid needs no name, no family, and no biography.\n\nLore: Codex background across editions treats every Tyranid bioform as disposable biomass the Hive Fleet will reclaim and regrow into something else entirely, and Black Library's Hive Fleet material never shows a Tyranid organism with anything resembling individual identity — only the Hive Mind's single, continuous intent wearing whichever body a given battle calls for.\n\nDesign: 0 points, documentation only; see notes for why this needs no separate purchase.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "t-k2XDsf1u_2bE7Hd",
					"name": "Adapted Biology (Resistant to Disease and Toxins)",
					"reference": "B81",
					"local_notes": "Resistant (Very Common: Disease, Toxins, Alien Biochemistry), Immunity.\n\nLore: A Tyranid organism can be grown to digest, and fight through, almost any biosphere or biochemical hazard a target world can offer — part of the same adaptive, purpose-grown biology that lets the Hive Fleet consume entire ecosystems. Consistent Codex background across editions; Deathwatch's Mark of the Xenos material treats Tyranid immunity to conventional toxins and pathogens as a given for any Imperial biologist studying a captured specimen.\n\nDesign: Resistant (Very Common), Immunity tier (B81): base value 30 for the broadest tabled hazard category, x1 for full Immunity = 30 points, the same tier and math this project's Custodes and Necron \"Resistant to Metabolic Hazards\" entries use for an adapted body that shrugs off disease, poison, and radiation alike.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mo9-2-OdR1pDrWcsM",
							"name": "Very Common: Disease, Toxins, Alien Atmospheres and Biochemistries",
							"reference": "B81",
							"cost_adj": "30"
						},
						{
							"id": "mULXI6o3FyvGPIFyZ",
							"name": "Immunity",
							"reference": "B81",
							"cost_adj": "x1"
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "T9QwnvZEeGrZmHf8N",
			"name": "Body Lens: Carnifex",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Carnifex: a living battering ram grown purely to soak up and deal out damage that would kill anything else outright, deployed wherever the swarm needs a hole punched through armour, fortification or a defensive line. Consistent Codex background across editions and Black Library's Hive Fleet fiction alike.\n\nDesign: 835 (Attribute Modifiers) + 750 (Chitin DR 150) + 50 (Regeneration, Fast) + 75 (Injury Tolerance 3) = 1710 points, added to Tyranid Biology's 20 for 1730 points of physiology alone before skills and weapon-symbiotes -- squarely in framework.md's Heroes and Legends tier (1,000-3,000), matching a Hive Tyrant's own physiology cost almost exactly despite the very different build (raw ST and DR instead of IQ, synapse range and wings; now well above it at ST 120). See the Carnifex template. NPC creature stat block only; this total is for GM reference, not party balance.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t2zqvS8siLrzJ4cWD",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Carnifex)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: SM +2, ST 120, DX 10, IQ 4, HT 16, HP 120. Increased ST +110 (B14, 10/level, 1,100) for ST 120, with the SM +2 discount: 1,100 x 0.8 = 880. ST 120 (user direction: a Carnifex should flip a tank with little struggle; Basic Lift 2,880 lb, and its two-handed shove or lift handles a light vehicle). DX 10 is human baseline (0 points) -- a Carnifex is not precise, it is simply unstoppable. Decreased IQ -6 (B16, 20/level, -120) for IQ 4, as debased as a Termagant's -- pure directed muscle. Increased HT +6 (60) for HT 16. HP 120, the ST-120 base (the earlier +10 extra HP dropped when ST doubled). Increased Basic Move +3 (B17, 5/level, 15): Basic Speed 6.5 gives Move 6, so +3 lands Basic Move at 9. 880-120+60+15 = 835.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 835,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "sm",
							"amount": 2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 110
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -6
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 6
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 3
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tadgNwsU4j2OSZw2H",
					"name": "Chitin (Carnifex)",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 150, all locations -- the anchor's own top figure. Weak Points 3 (the thickest, least gappy chitin any Tyranid grows, on a par with Terminator plate): on a hit that fails to penetrate, roll 3d; 3 or less halves DR (75). Eye clusters run at DR 75 rather than 150.\n\nLore: A Tyranid's exoskeleton is grown, not worn: dense bio-ceramic chitin fused directly to the flesh beneath it, thickest on the largest bioforms and thin enough on a Termagant to be more like a swarm-trooper's flak jacket than real armour plate. Universal across Codex background and every piece of Black Library Tyranid fiction.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance (B46, 5/level, all locations): natural DR bought directly on the body, per framework.md's explicit instruction that chitin is a character trait, not equipment. Each body-type lens overrides `levels`/`dr` to its own figure from framework.md's Tyranid anchor table; see that lens's own copy of this trait for its point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 150,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 150
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tZ9uf1m0unMqunSId",
					"name": "Regeneration (Adapted Biomass, Fast)",
					"reference": "B80",
					"local_notes": "Regeneration (Fast): recovers 1 HP per minute even without rest or first aid.\n\nLore: A Carnifex is built to absorb an amount of damage that would kill any other bioform several times over and keep coming, closing wounds visibly fast enough to matter mid-fight — Codex background's consistent framing of the Carnifex as the swarm's most durable single-target answer to armour.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Regeneration (Fast) (B80, 50 points), one tier above the Hive Tyrant's Regular rate, matching the Carnifex's specific reputation for shrugging off damage that would down a Hive Tyrant.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"base_points": 50
				},
				{
					"id": "tifXPZCxq1rdaEZ6X",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction)",
					"reference": "P53",
					"local_notes": "Divisor 3 (level = divisor): after DR and wounding multipliers, all injury is divided by 3 (round down, minimum 1 if anything penetrated).\n\nLore: A Carnifex is a mountain of muscle and redundant organs that simply does not stop. Large synapse-beasts and monsters shrug off wounds that would fell smaller creatures.\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) (GURPS Powers p. 53, 25 points per level, level = divisor): divisor 3 = 75 points. Gives the Carnifex the bulk-and-redundant-organs toughness the lore describes on top of its chitin DR and extra HP.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 25,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "t0PaFn8t0pPLQIH1k",
			"name": "Monstrous Crushing Claws",
			"reference": "B88",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A pair of massive crushing pincers grown in place of scything talons on some Carnifexes and Hive Tyrants, built to pulp armour and machinery outright rather than cut through it — the bio-weapon most often shown in Codex: Tyranids background and Black Library's Tyranid-focused fiction alike caving in a Dreadnought's hull or snapping a Rhino's axle in one blow.\n\nDesign: Framework.md's own anchor: \"Monstrous crushing claws | thr+6d(5) cr.\" Built at a Carnifex's own ST 60 (thr 7d-1): Crushing Striker (B88, 5 points) with Armor Divisor (5) (B102, +150%) = 5x2.5=12.5, rounded to 13, for the thr+0(5) baseline; the anchor's own +6d is bought as Innate Attack (Crush) (B61, 5/die) at 6 dice = 30, with Armor Divisor (5) (+150%) and Melee Attack, Reach C (-30%): net +120%, 30x2.2=66. Total 13+66=79. Checked by exact dice distribution at ST 60 (thr+6d(5) = 13d-1(5)): averages 44.5 and clears every personal-armour DR up to and including Astartes plate at 100%, dropping to 74.0% against a Terminator (DR 200, eff. 40) — the hardest-hitting personal-scale melee weapon in this file against the very heaviest infantry armour. Against vehicles: AV10 100%, AV11 98.9%, AV12 74.0%, AV13 3.7%, AV14 0% — genuinely \"cracks a Dreadnought or a Rhino,\" matching the anchor and the fiction, while still falling short of a main battle tank's frontal glacis, which is exactly the gap between this and a true anti-tank symbiote (the Heavy Venom Cannon or Stranglethorn Cannon above).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"base_points": 79,
			"weapons": [
				{
					"id": "wLjujGoFllclYmBw6",
					"sv": 1,
					"damage": {
						"type": "cr",
						"st": "thr",
						"base": "6d",
						"armor_divisor": 5
					},
					"reach": "1",
					"parry": "0",
					"usage": "Standard",
					"usage_notes": "Written for a Carnifex-scale bearer (ST 60, thr 7d-1, giving a total of 13d-1(5) cr); rebuild the +6d Innate Attack component for any other bearer's own ST per this file's header note.",
					"defaults": [
						{
							"type": "dx",
							"modifier": 0
						},
						{
							"type": "skill",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Brawling"
							}
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "t66V-Vl7TwMp4Mr6m",
			"name": "Venom Cannon",
			"reference": "B61",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A heavy bio-cannon that hurls a dense, explosive mass of caustic bile at a single target hard enough to crack light armour outright — the swarm's first real answer to armoured infantry and light vehicles, most often seen mounted on Tyranid Warriors and Carnifexes. Codex: Tyranids background across editions and Deathwatch RPG material both describe it as capable of blowing an unarmoured target apart and staggering even power-armoured targets with a solid hit.\n\nDesign: Framework.md's own anchor: \"Venom cannon | 6d×3(3) cr ex.\" Innate Attack (Crush) (B61, 5/die) at an effective 18 dice (6 base x3 multiplier) = 90, with Explosion (B102, +50%, for the \"ex\" bursting bile) and Armor Divisor (3) (B102, +100%): net +150%, 90x2.5=225. Checked by exact dice distribution: 6dx3(3) averages 63 and penetrates flak (DR 30, eff. 10) 100%, carapace (DR 70, eff. 23.3) 100%, Sororitas plate (DR 85, eff. 28.3) 99.8%, Astartes plate (DR 100, eff. 33.3) 99.0%, and Terminator plate (DR 200, eff. 66.7) 36.3% — genuinely \"cracks armour,\" including a real chance against Terminator-grade plate. Against vehicles: AV10 99.0%, AV11 85.5%, AV12 36.3%, AV13 0.99%, AV14 0% — a real light-vehicle killer, no threat at all to a main battle tank, exactly the light-anti-armour niche the anchor implies without inflating it into a lascannon. Handling: Acc 4 and 200/600 range, this project's own heavy-symbiote estimate; Shots 4(60), a one-minute regrowth for the biggest single discharge in the venom-gland family.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"base_points": 225,
			"weapons": [
				{
					"id": "Wk8WhUEsSYxuhe0Bd",
					"sv": 1,
					"damage": {
						"type": "cr ex",
						"base": "6dx3",
						"armor_divisor": 3
					},
					"accuracy": "4",
					"range": "200/600",
					"rate_of_fire": "1",
					"shots": "4(60)",
					"usage": "Standard",
					"usage_notes": "ST-independent, grown as a torso- or arm-mounted organ (Tyranid Warrior/Carnifex-scale bioforms only). Shots regrow over the listed time as the bearer's bile-gland refills.",
					"defaults": [
						{
							"type": "skill",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Innate Attack"
							},
							"specialization": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Projectile"
							}
						},
						{
